{
  "world_updates": [
    {
      "type": "NPC_SPOKE"
    }
  ],
  "memory_summary": "Old Fletcher shares more of his life and trade with the wanderer after being asked about himself.",
  "new_entities": [],
  "rule_flags": [],
  "narration": "Old Fletcher leans back against the rough-hewn table, one gnarled hand resting on the haft of a half-finished bow. His eyes, sharp despite the years, study the wanderer as if weighing what has been said.",
  "npc_response": "You've heard my trade, but not my life, have you? I was born in a village that no longer exists, where the forest swallowed the church and the men learned to shoot before they learned to pray. I left when I was sixteen, with a bow and a pack, and I have walked ever since. I have made my living from the edge of the world, fletching arrows for whoever would pay and cutting wood for whoever would take it. I have seen kings come and go, and I have seen the same old faces in the garrison every winter. I am not proud of it. I am not proud of much. But I have never lied to a man who asked me straight, and I have never forgotten a face that gave me honest coin."
